\begin{proof}\tH\ (Text v40, review fix18 F-5.) By Lemma~\ref{U:lem:max}, $\Phi$ is a smooth solution of $\partial_tf=-\nabla_s^2\kappa$ on $[0,T_{\max})$ (an \fn{IsFlow} at $n=1$ is such a solution, Lemma~\ref{U:lem:vel}), every smooth solution from $F$ on an interval $[0,T]$ is a restriction of $\Phi$, and $\Phi$ has no smooth extension to $[0,T_{\max}]$ when $T_{\max}<\infty$. So $\Phi$ is a maximal solution in the sense of DKS, read as ``maximal among smooth solutions'' (author item A-2). If $T_{\max}<\infty$, (D2) gives $\int_{\gamma(t_1+t)}k^2ds\ge c(T_{\max}-t)^{-1/4}\to\infty$ as $t\uparrow T_{\max}$.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}[the route of text v39, through (D1)]\label{U:rem:D1}
Alternatively: if $\sup\int k^2ds<\infty$, (D1) extends $\Phi$ smoothly to $[0,T_{\max}]$; the extension is an \fn{IsFlow} on $[0,T_{\max}]$ (the velocity identity passes to the limit $t\uparrow T_{\max}$ with all derivatives, and $\Phi(T_{\max})$ is an immersion because DKS's extension is a smooth solution, in particular $|\partial_x\Phi|>0$ up to $T_{\max}$), contradicting Lemma~\ref{U:lem:max}. This uses a step inside DKS's proof rather than its statement, and the immersion at $T_{\max}$ (the C-4 completion); the statement (D2) needs neither.
\end{remark}
\noindent This is (P1$'$) with: existence and uniqueness from (L1), (L2); the criterion from the statement of DKS Theorem~3.1, (D2) (text v40; text v39 used (D1)).

